{# One map, drawn the same way on all five pages that have one.

   Parameters: `markers`, and optionally `paths`, `zoom` and `label`.
   A marker is {lat, lon} and may carry a `colour`; a path is a list of the
   same. Where the numbers come from and which way round they go is
   View\MapView's business, not this template's.

   Two renderings of the one map, and only one of them is ever fetched. The div
   is what public/js/map.js turns into a map you can pan; the picture inside
   `<noscript>` is what a crawler and a scriptless browser get instead of an
   empty box. A browser running scripts never requests the picture, so it costs
   nothing to keep -- and it is the reason these pages still have indexable,
   described map content now that the map itself is a canvas.

   Nothing at all without a token: map_config() returns an empty string, and a
   page with no map is better than a page of broken ones.

   The container's height is in the stylesheet rather than measured from the
   map, so the page has its final shape before any of this loads and does not
   jump when it does. #}
{% set paths = paths|default([]) %}
{% set zoom = zoom|default(null) %}
{% set config = map_config(markers, paths, zoom) %}

{% if config %}
    {# `group` rather than `region`: it wants a name a screen reader can hear
       and skip, without adding a landmark to every one of these pages. What is
       inside it is interactive, so it is not `img`. #}
    <div class="place__map" data-map="{{ config }}"
         role="group" aria-label="{{ label|default('Map') }}"></div>

    <noscript>
        <img class="place__map" src="{{ map_image(markers, paths, zoom) }}"
             width="650" height="300" alt="{{ label|default('Map') }}">
    </noscript>
{% endif %}
